WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.402
Well, it sounds like you want to treat it
like a legal product.

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:05.238
Or a better way forward, which is just decriminalize it.

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:07.474
This looks like a debate.

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:08.675
It's illegal!

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:09.776
That's not true.

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:10.777
But it's not.

00:00:10.777 --> 00:00:13.079
Yes, these two advocates 
fundamentally disagree

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:14.714
about a major policy issue.

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:16.383
I support the legalization

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:18.718
and regulation of cannabis for adults.

00:00:18.985 --> 00:00:21.187
I oppose legalization of cannabis.

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:23.356
We could have just had them 
show up and fight it out.

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:25.425
That's how these things usually go.

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:26.426
"No, I think..."

00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:27.660
But when you watch people argue

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.829
are you judging content or style?

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:31.931
And how do you know if they're 
even telling the truth?

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:35.402
So we wanted to try something different.

00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:37.170
We asked both Paul and Will

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.773
to send us five facts about 
cannabis legalization

00:00:39.773 --> 00:00:42.308
that their opponent would 
have to concede are true.

00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:44.310
They fact checked each other.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.745
We fact checked them, too.

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:48.048
And after going back and forth 
on the exact phrasing

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:49.749
we landed on 6.

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:51.985
Do you both agree that these are true?

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:54.020
Yes. Yes, I do.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.289
They'll each present their facts 
and get to respond

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:58.258
to the other's facts with a footnote.

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.560
Okay, here we go.

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:09.035
National polls consistently show...

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:12.238
that a majority of the 
American public support

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:13.873
legalizing marijuana.

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:18.111
In 1969, a minority supported legalization.

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:20.980
Today, it's nearly 70%.

00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:25.051
As more states have adopted legalization

00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:28.922
support among the public 
has risen in parallel.

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:31.157
There's no buyer's remorse.

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:33.526
My footnote to that would be

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:36.196
these polls often give what we believe

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:38.131
to be a false dichotomy of choice.

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:40.567
If I have to choose

00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:43.403
between arrest and incarceration 
or legalization

00:01:43.403 --> 00:01:45.238
I'm going to choose legalization as well.

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:47.373
But when we dig a little bit deeper
and there's some

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:50.009
national polls from Emerson that 
show that support

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:52.846
for legalization falls well below 50%

00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:55.081
when people are presented 
with other options

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:56.850
such as decriminalization

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:58.918
and the medicinal use of the cannabis plant.

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.887
Well, let's talk about those polls, Will.

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:03.857
Even when you provide more choices

00:02:03.857 --> 00:02:10.263
the most popular choice is 
legalizing marijuana by adults.

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:14.134
My name is Will Jones.

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:16.236
I'm the director of community engagement 
and outreach

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:17.637
at Smart Approaches to Marijuana.

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:21.407
I really believe that we can 
explore better policy options

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:24.444
when it comes to marijuana policy
than either criminalization...

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:26.379
or commercialization.

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:29.282
Decriminalization removes penalties

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:32.785
for the personal possession and use of marijuana.

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:36.523
Legalization and commercialization 
would allow for businesses

00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:38.424
to market and sell marijuana.

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:40.927
And so that's the primary difference between the two.

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:45.398
A recent study reported

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:48.001
higher rates of fatal car crashes in states

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:50.470
that had legal recreational marijuana.

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:53.573
We have to look at, what 
are all the areas

00:02:53.573 --> 00:02:56.075
of society and of public health 
and safety

00:02:56.075 --> 00:02:57.510
that are going to be impacted

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.812
through legalization and commercialization?

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:03.449
In fact, the study you cited 
looked at a subset of states.

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:06.553
Even the authors of this 
study acknowledge...

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:07.820
it may have nothing to do

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.123
with the change in the status 
of marijuana at all.

00:03:10.557 --> 00:03:12.992
That there's a number of 
different confounders.

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:17.497
Fortunately, we have real world 
experience in this country

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:19.966
reducing rates of drunk driving.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.503
I am confident that we can continue

00:03:23.503 --> 00:03:26.172
to reduce rates of drug driving.

00:03:26.773 --> 00:03:29.609
When you commercialize something, 
you have advertising.

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:30.543
You have marketing.

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:31.511
That increases use.

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:33.146
That's what it's supposed to do.

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:34.781
And when you have 
more people using,

00:03:34.781 --> 00:03:37.250
that percentage of people 
that use irresponsibly

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:40.320
is going to represent a larger 
number of people.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.889
Do we know whether use of 
marijuana goes up

00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:44.057
after legalization?

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:46.292
It tends to go up in people

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.962
that are about 26.

00:03:49.229 --> 00:03:51.397
And so, yes.

00:03:51.397 --> 00:03:54.667
The vision here is that we can 
craft policy with marijuana

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:56.536
where we don't create something that

00:03:56.536 --> 00:03:58.471
creates a negative public health impact

00:03:58.471 --> 00:03:59.772
that we then have to address

00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:01.774
with massive campaigns afterwards.

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.312
My name is Paul Armentano.

00:04:06.312 --> 00:04:10.016
I always believed strongly 
in civil liberties.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:14.921
In a world that doesn't just 
accept alcohol

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:17.890
but celebrates the use of alcohol.

00:04:18.057 --> 00:04:21.794
It is unjust to be 
ruining people's lives 

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:24.097
over their use of a plant.

00:04:27.033 --> 00:04:28.901
Police in the United States

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:34.173
still make hundreds of thousands of low level 
marijuana arrests every year.

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:38.811
Over 90% of those arrests are 
for simple possession.

00:04:38.845 --> 00:04:41.381
And the results of these arrests 
are significant.

00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:43.616
People lose their jobs.

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:46.052
People lose their professional licensure

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:47.854
their right to vote

00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:49.822
to possess a gun

00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:51.491
subsidized housing.

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:54.927
In some states, they even lose the right 
to adopt a child.

00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:59.632
And those arrested disproportionately 
are young people

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:01.668
poor people and people of color.

00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:04.270
So I agree 100% that

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.907
there is systemic inequality and injustice

00:05:07.907 --> 00:05:10.343
in the way that the enforcement 
of marijuana laws

00:05:10.343 --> 00:05:11.911
has been carried out in our country.

00:05:11.911 --> 00:05:14.580
But when we look at the data
from states that have legalized

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.217
we see that systemic inequality 
in law enforcement

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:21.788
even around the laws of legalization, 
continues to exist.

00:05:21.788 --> 00:05:24.057
We still continue to see disparities.

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:25.191
Absolutely.

00:05:25.191 --> 00:05:28.161
Nobody ever said legalizing cannabis 
was going to end 

00:05:28.161 --> 00:05:31.397
systemic racism or even 
racism in policing.

00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:35.702
But it certainly removes a major 
tool from that toolbox.

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:38.037
And we've seen in states that have legalized

00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:43.343
total arrests for marijuana 
dropped significantly.

00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:47.280
A 2017 survey found that

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.883
81% of marijuana business owners 
in the U.S. are white.

00:05:51.417 --> 00:05:54.454
This is a huge contrast to the people

00:05:54.454 --> 00:05:56.689
that are most often talked about when we hear

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:59.492
about marijuana legalization,
which is entrepreneurs of color

00:05:59.492 --> 00:06:01.928
and reinvesting in communities harmed 
by the war on drugs.

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:03.563
That's a great idea.

00:06:03.563 --> 00:06:06.032
But that's not the reality of how 
it's panning out at all.

00:06:06.232 --> 00:06:08.334
The other concern is that the placement

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:11.404
of marijuana businesses 
is disproportionately

00:06:11.404 --> 00:06:12.572
in communities of color

00:06:12.572 --> 00:06:14.340
just like we see with liquor stores.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.143
We all want to see greater inclusion

00:06:17.143 --> 00:06:19.245
in the commercial marijuana industry.

00:06:19.479 --> 00:06:23.483
Of course, one of the reasons 
we don't have that diversity now

00:06:23.483 --> 00:06:26.619
is because the federal law treats cannabis

00:06:26.619 --> 00:06:28.955
as a Schedule I controlled substance...

00:06:29.122 --> 00:06:32.525
which means that banks and 
other financial institutions

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:36.262
will not provide lending, 
will not provide loans

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:38.464
to these would-be entrepreneurs

00:06:38.464 --> 00:06:40.666
to allow them to get their foot in the door.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:46.239
In fact, we have a potential fix:
the Safe Banking Act.

00:06:46.539 --> 00:06:49.308
But your organization, Will, 
opposes

00:06:49.308 --> 00:06:51.344
that change in federal policy

00:06:51.344 --> 00:06:53.913
which would help remedy 
this very situation.

00:06:54.547 --> 00:06:57.450
Yeah, and I think that's because 
the Safe Banking Act

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.887
would open the floodgates to 
huge tobacco companies

00:07:00.887 --> 00:07:03.689
and alcohol companies totally 
taking over the industry.

00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:05.558
And it's a great idea that we want 
to have smaller

00:07:05.558 --> 00:07:07.293
entrepreneurs get into this.

00:07:07.293 --> 00:07:09.429
But we know how business 
works in the US.

00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:12.098
We know how capitalism works. 
On multiple fronts

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:14.967
we're going to continue to see 
this inequality exist.

00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:22.008
Decriminalization leaves the unregulated 
marketplace intact.

00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:26.412
And it continues to perpetuate 
police interaction

00:07:26.412 --> 00:07:27.980
with the community.

00:07:27.980 --> 00:07:31.050
It shouldn't have to perpetuate 
law enforcement.

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:33.619
I think that we can craft 
decriminalization in a way

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:35.121
that completely removes it

00:07:35.121 --> 00:07:36.589
from the criminal justice system

00:07:36.589 --> 00:07:38.191
and treats it more as a health issue.

00:07:38.191 --> 00:07:42.094
I think like Portugal has done a great job 
at de-stigmatizing use

00:07:42.094 --> 00:07:45.031
and for people to have problematic 
use to have services

00:07:45.031 --> 00:07:46.499
provided for them.

00:07:46.499 --> 00:07:48.901
When you say that 
decriminalization

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:52.672
perpetuates law enforcement contact 
with marijuana users and sellers

00:07:52.672 --> 00:07:54.240
can you explain why that would be?

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.709
If it's not a crime, why are the police involved?

00:07:56.943 --> 00:07:59.145
Because marijuana is illicit.

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:00.913
Which means it's contraband.

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:03.449
So if the police encounter somebody

00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:05.585
possessing or using cannabis

00:08:05.585 --> 00:08:11.457
they have a legal responsibility to interact, 
to seize that cannabis.

00:08:11.691 --> 00:08:13.860
They also can continue

00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:18.030
to search individuals, pull them over, 
frisk them

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:20.466
because they claim they smelled cannabis

00:08:20.466 --> 00:08:23.536
because, again, cannabis itself

00:08:23.536 --> 00:08:26.472
is an illicit substance, 
not a legal one.

00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:28.040
And if a decriminalization law

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.843
allows for that, then it's a poorly crafted 
decriminalization law.

00:08:30.843 --> 00:08:33.513
Will, it sounds like you want Washington DC.

00:08:33.513 --> 00:08:35.147
People can possess marijuana.

00:08:35.147 --> 00:08:36.582
They can grow marijuana.

00:08:36.582 --> 00:08:39.218
But there's no regulated 
commercial market

00:08:39.218 --> 00:08:41.120
for people to obtain marijuana.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:43.823
And that's why we have 
unregulated shops

00:08:43.823 --> 00:08:48.294
gifting marijuana products, 
selling unregulated products.

00:08:49.395 --> 00:08:52.732
Is that a preferable policy to one

00:08:52.732 --> 00:08:55.468
where the commercial market 
is licensed and regulated?

00:08:55.868 --> 00:08:57.336
I do think it's preferable

00:08:57.336 --> 00:08:59.906
but I think that is because the shops 
came into place

00:08:59.906 --> 00:09:02.475
with the understanding that eventually 
it would be commercialized.

00:09:02.475 --> 00:09:03.809
Even more ideally:

00:09:03.809 --> 00:09:06.812
We create a new world, 
a new concept where it's like

00:09:06.812 --> 00:09:08.080
Hey, grow this yourself.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.750
Or if you want to have it and share it
with friends, do that.

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:14.720
But this is not something that we're 
going to allow industries to sell.

00:09:17.557 --> 00:09:19.292
Big tobacco and alcohol companies

00:09:19.292 --> 00:09:21.427
have invested billions of dollars

00:09:21.427 --> 00:09:24.664
into the cannabis industry and 
are lobbying for legalization

00:09:24.664 --> 00:09:26.365
at the federal and state levels.

00:09:26.365 --> 00:09:28.334
And this is especially concerning

00:09:28.334 --> 00:09:31.337
because we know the track record 
that these companies

00:09:31.337 --> 00:09:32.738
have of addiction-for-profit

00:09:32.738 --> 00:09:35.575
whether we're talking about alcohol
or the tobacco industries.

00:09:35.575 --> 00:09:37.310
It raises some concerns for public health

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:38.711
that I think are very important.

00:09:38.711 --> 00:09:41.213
Certainly there are commercial interests.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:44.684
But to imply that they are the ones 
driving this train

00:09:44.684 --> 00:09:46.118
is simply disingenuous.

00:09:46.352 --> 00:09:50.790
This is an issue that was largely 
pushed on the grassroots level...

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:53.426
20, 30 years ago.

00:09:53.492 --> 00:09:55.294
And it remains the grassroots level

00:09:55.294 --> 00:09:57.697
that's pushing this issue today.

00:09:57.697 --> 00:10:00.032
Not corporations, 
not big business

00:10:00.032 --> 00:10:01.901
but in fact, the people.

00:10:02.368 --> 00:10:04.971
Whoever has pushed this for 20 years,
at the end of the day

00:10:04.971 --> 00:10:07.073
there's big tobacco and 
alcohol companies

00:10:07.073 --> 00:10:09.542
standing by, ready to profit off of that.

00:10:09.542 --> 00:10:10.810
And that's a big concern.

00:10:15.114 --> 00:10:18.484
I think Will's proposed solutions

00:10:18.484 --> 00:10:22.622
policies like decriminalization,
don't go far enough...

00:10:23.422 --> 00:10:25.257
because at the end of the day

00:10:25.257 --> 00:10:28.127
if we don't want criminal entrepreneurs

00:10:28.127 --> 00:10:30.396
being the ones providing cannabis

00:10:30.396 --> 00:10:33.332
if we want purity of products

00:10:33.332 --> 00:10:36.235
if we want products that are 
labeled and regulated...

00:10:36.602 --> 00:10:39.505
then we have to have legalization.

00:10:39.905 --> 00:10:44.110
Because we can't impose 
regulations and rules

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:46.379
on a decriminalized substance.

00:10:46.646 --> 00:10:49.715
What this discussion has showed 
is that we need to move

00:10:49.715 --> 00:10:53.152
past the punitive policies that 
we've had for marijuana

00:10:53.152 --> 00:10:54.654
in the past years in our country

00:10:54.654 --> 00:10:57.423
while still avoiding the 
excesses of commercialization.

00:10:57.523 --> 00:11:01.894
What you see from continued habitual
use for heavy users

00:11:01.894 --> 00:11:04.997
that's where you start to see 
the negative public health costs

00:11:04.997 --> 00:11:05.965
whether that's on driving

00:11:05.965 --> 00:11:08.367
or whether that's on mental health,
whether that's on youth use.

00:11:08.534 --> 00:11:10.836
We have to move forward in a way 
that isn't going to allow

00:11:10.836 --> 00:11:13.572
for the exploitation of 
vulnerable communities 

00:11:13.572 --> 00:11:14.940
by addiction for profit companies.

00:11:16.575 --> 00:11:17.176
Let's cut.

00:11:18.878 --> 00:11:19.712
So that's it.

00:11:19.712 --> 00:11:21.414
That's our take on a debate.

00:11:21.414 --> 00:11:23.883
Big thanks to Paul Armentano and Will Jones

00:11:23.883 --> 00:11:25.818
for participating in this experiment.

00:11:26.519 --> 00:11:28.554
If there are other topics you'd like to see

00:11:28.554 --> 00:11:31.190
please let us know in the comments.

